text:
ashley: Heads upran into a TS typing regression when the JS SDK is consumed from the Edge (ESM) bundle. Generic overloads collapse to any after our ESM packaging step. Repro repo: https://github.com/redwood/repro-edge-typing-minimal

kevin: can you paste the smallest repro snippet?

ashley: minimal SDK API:
```ts
// sdk/src/index.ts
export function call<T>(arg: T): Promise<{ out: T }>;
export function call(arg: unknown) { return Promise.resolve({ out: arg as any }); }
```
consumer:
```ts
import { call } from '@redwood/sdk';
type Req = { id: string };
async function go(){
  const r = await call<Req>({ id: 'x' });
  // in Edge ESM bundle r.out is typed as any
  r.out. // no completion
}
```

ashley: Node/CJS consumer gets correct inference. Only when app imports our published ESM tarball (esbuild/rollup output, tsconfig moduleResolution=bundler) do overloads disappear.

maria: did you diff the generated d.ts files between cjs and esm outputs? that often explains whether TS emit or our rollup re-exporting is to blame.

ashley: yes. cjs dist has the overloads preserved in index.d.ts; the esm entry's index.d.ts shows a single widened signature (arg: any). The ESM entry is a tiny re-export wrapper that does `export * from './runtime'`.

kevin: classic packaging footgun. re-exports can cause declaration merging to lose overloads if declarations are aggregated differently. two quick options: ship a hand-authored single index.d.ts that declares all overloads, or change build to produce an explicit esm index.d.ts that preserves the originals.

maria: also check tsconfig flags like preserveValueImports, declarationMap, and composite. If the emitter is run only on the wrapper file it may not see the original overload declarations.

ashley: created minimal test that compiles a tiny app against the published tarball and fails. I'll attach to the issue.
\
dev-bot: Created ticket RW-DEVEX-1995: "ESM packaging collapses SDK overloads for Edge consumers" (triaged).

kevin: plan: 1) add CI job that emits declarations for both exports and compares public surface; 2) switch esm package entry to point at compiled file that includes d.ts directly (avoid re-export wrapper); 3) if needed, ship a curated index.d.ts for the public API with explicit overloads.

maria: short-term doc note: recommend Node/CJS import or pin version that includes correct d.ts. I'll PR a small troubleshooting snippet in SDK README.

ashley: thanks. posted repro and test case in the ticket. I'll follow up with a patch that changes packaging and adds the CI validation. :sparkles:
